WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.210
Rules don't matter in language
learning, at least not the

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.040
rules that are taught at school.

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:08.670
I'm going to talk about that because I
just finished reading a book written by

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.870
Manfred Spitzer, whom I have mentioned
before, called "The Mind Within the Net"

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:16.800
where he covers how the brain learns,
what happens when we learn, and it's

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.880
kind of a very good follow up
to a previous book I read by Manfred

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.620
Spitzer in German called "Lernen"

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.240
I like Manfred Spitzer.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.720
He's very good at explaining what
happens in our brains when we learn.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:34.650
But this latest book is particularly
interesting because much of language

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.320
instruction is based on teaching rules.

00:00:37.435 --> 00:00:40.015
That is largely counterproductive.

00:00:40.195 --> 00:00:44.935
Spitzer begins his book with the
example of how babies, or young kids,

00:00:44.965 --> 00:00:47.545
learn the past tense of English verbs.

00:00:47.575 --> 00:00:52.315
Surprisingly, they first learn some
of the very common irregular verbs

00:00:52.405 --> 00:00:57.355
for the simple reason that those verbs
are very common: went, came, et cetera.

00:00:57.355 --> 00:01:00.145
And so they learn these and by
learning these, and by hearing other

00:01:00.285 --> 00:01:00.764
verbs,

00:01:00.764 --> 00:01:05.084
they gradually start to develop
some rules, some patterns for

00:01:05.084 --> 00:01:06.825
how the verbs work in English.

00:01:06.854 --> 00:01:10.905
And so after learning a few irregular
verbs, because they're the most

00:01:10.905 --> 00:01:15.315
frequent, then they become quite good
at learning the sort of regular verbs.

00:01:15.315 --> 00:01:17.804
That is where the -ed is added to the verb.

00:01:17.835 --> 00:01:20.384
And studies have shown that
adults learn the same way.

00:01:20.414 --> 00:01:24.554
And then as they develop this pattern,
because they hear more and more of

00:01:24.554 --> 00:01:26.850
these, verbs with the -ed attached them.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:30.990
So they begin to understand that the
past tense is formed by adding -ed.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.430
Then they start to apply the -ed past
tense to verbs that don't take the -ed.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.320
In other words, other irregular verbs.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.580
So after that initial period where
the very common irregular verbs

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.280
are learned, they then go through a
period where they're very good with

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.900
the regular verbs that take -ed, and
then subsequently they learn the not

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.270
so frequent verbs that don't take -ed.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:52.320
That is the pattern.

00:01:52.515 --> 00:01:55.904
That is the pattern for kids, and
it's the pattern for adult learners.

00:01:55.934 --> 00:01:56.774
What does that tell us?

00:01:56.835 --> 00:01:59.865
It tells us that it is not
a matter of teaching a rule.

00:01:59.895 --> 00:02:05.024
It's a matter of exposure to the language,
allowing the brain to form its own rules.

00:02:05.054 --> 00:02:07.005
He again illustrates this point...

00:02:07.214 --> 00:02:09.884
Well, first of all, he talks
about the father trying to teach

00:02:09.889 --> 00:02:12.945
the child, you know, "This is
how the past tense is formed."

00:02:12.975 --> 00:02:13.965
That's unnecessary.

00:02:13.965 --> 00:02:17.805
If the father is speaking in a loud
voice and pointing his finger at the

00:02:17.805 --> 00:02:22.185
child, the child will only form the the
following pattern: that is, my father

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.310
always talks in a loud voice and points
his finger. Or he may deduce another

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:29.280
pattern that all fathers talk in a
loud voice and point their finger.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.459
The rule that matters is how the
language is used, and that is

00:02:32.459 --> 00:02:34.560
acquired from interaction with others.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.630
In fact, one of the important things in
childhood is the ability to play, because

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:43.890
play for the child is an opportunity, and
is very important from an evolutionary

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.450
perspective, where the child can

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:50.305
make mistakes, interact, experiment
with different things, and start to form

00:02:50.305 --> 00:02:53.395
patterns as to, you know, how life works.

00:02:53.815 --> 00:02:56.725
Learn to anticipate what's
going to happen in his or her life.

00:02:56.785 --> 00:03:00.355
And it's done in an environment
which is relatively safe.

00:03:00.415 --> 00:03:02.185
It's not like you take the child out,

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:08.170
you know, for caveman days, hunting where a
mistake can have catastrophic consequences

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:13.329
In play, it's basically a consequence
free opportunity to experiment.

00:03:13.329 --> 00:03:16.030
And speaking of experiments,
just to sort of contrast the

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.549
sort of rules-based language

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:24.450
production with the sort of naturally evolved
process of correct language production,

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.460
Spitzer takes the
example of text-to-speech.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.980
So there was a system of text-to-speech.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.290
The original text to speech was called
something like DECtalk, D-E-C talk.

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:36.990
Where this was used with English,

00:03:37.155 --> 00:03:41.265
there was a problem because, in Spanish,
the spelling is very consistent.

00:03:41.265 --> 00:03:45.495
For example, Spanish and other languages,
spelling is very consistent. In English,

00:03:45.495 --> 00:03:51.435
of course, spelling is notoriously
not consistent. So the solution was to

00:03:51.495 --> 00:03:57.125
develop a formula or an algorithm, which
looked at three letters on either side

00:03:57.125 --> 00:04:00.555
of the letter that they were trying
to develop a sound for in order to

00:04:00.555 --> 00:04:02.954
develop the correct sound for that word.

00:04:02.984 --> 00:04:08.070
But even with that rule, the process
was very slow. But as the technology

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:12.720
improved and people developing text-
to-speech were able to access massive

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.860
amounts of, you know, corpora, massive
amounts of language, content, audio,

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:22.200
text, audio, text, they found that it
was much faster and more accurate to

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:27.090
allow the computer, without any rule,
without any instruction, to develop its

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:33.240
own patterns, its own rules in order to
produce accurately text-to-speech from

00:04:33.345 --> 00:04:34.215
written English.

00:04:34.365 --> 00:04:37.035
And that's a bit like what
happens in our brains.

00:04:37.155 --> 00:04:41.175
We don't need specific instruction,
we just need to give the brain

00:04:41.175 --> 00:04:45.075
enough input in order for the brain
to develop the necessary patterns.

00:04:45.105 --> 00:04:51.015
And modeling neural networks has enabled
scientists to prove that unlike what

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:55.970
Chomsky said, where he seemed to
suggest that there isn't enough input

00:04:55.970 --> 00:05:00.650
for the brain to develop all the
necessary rules to deal with language.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:04.220
In fact, it is enough, and they
show how it actually happens.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:07.730
Leaving aside the fact that
not every language deals with

00:05:07.735 --> 00:05:09.110
past tense in the same way.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:12.860
I mean in in Chinese, the verb
actually doesn't even change form.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:17.690
And when the child is born, of course
the child can adapt to the patterns of

00:05:17.735 --> 00:05:22.745
any language, whether it be the sound
or the structure or how words function

00:05:22.745 --> 00:05:26.855
in a sentence. Spitzer says that
initially the potential is limitless.

00:05:26.915 --> 00:05:29.045
The capability is very
much limited of the child.

00:05:29.225 --> 00:05:32.975
As the child gets better at producing
the language, then the capability

00:05:32.975 --> 00:05:36.365
improves and the potential, the
range of sounds, the range of

00:05:36.375 --> 00:05:39.465
structures that the child can
deal with, becomes more limited, and

00:05:39.465 --> 00:05:45.225
as we get older, then we are less
capable of learning new languages to

00:05:45.255 --> 00:05:47.175
essentially native-like proficiency.

00:05:47.235 --> 00:05:50.925
But the principle is that it's not
rules that govern how we speak.

00:05:50.955 --> 00:05:55.605
So learning the rules, being instructed
in the rules is not going to help us speak.

00:05:55.635 --> 00:06:01.095
What we have to do is give our
brains enough input, enough exposure,

00:06:01.125 --> 00:06:03.735
enough experience, enough events.

00:06:03.945 --> 00:06:07.335
So that the brain can
gradually evolve its own rules.

00:06:07.365 --> 00:06:11.955
And these rules are more efficient than
any rules that we attempt to teach.

00:06:12.075 --> 00:06:15.405
Spitzer goes into a lot of
detail about the hippocampus and the

00:06:15.409 --> 00:06:20.775
cortex, which is kind of beyond my
knowledge base, beyond my pay grade.

00:06:20.835 --> 00:06:24.164
But the gist of it is, as I
understand it, and some of you out

00:06:24.164 --> 00:06:27.945
there may be more knowledgeable,
that short-term memory, even medium-

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:33.590
term memory events are stored in the
hippocampus, whereas in the cortex, which

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:38.599
surrounds the brain, there's a much
slower process of gradually acquiring

00:06:38.659 --> 00:06:42.950
skills, longer-term knowledge, skills,
and language, and so forth and so on.

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:48.140
And that the hippocampus is kind
of the instructor of the cortex by

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:52.280
giving constant events: a new word,
a new sentence, new sounds.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:57.120
So these things are constantly and
repetitively pushed towards the cortex

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:02.820
and the cortex gradually converts this
into the sort of explicit knowledge, the

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:07.890
ability to do something, as opposed to
our ability to remember specific events.

00:07:08.145 --> 00:07:12.104
In fact, we don't retain them
in our hippocampus that long.

00:07:12.135 --> 00:07:14.655
The hippocampus has to pass
them along to the cortex.

00:07:14.684 --> 00:07:18.885
And so the important thing is to
keep bombarding the hippocampus with

00:07:18.885 --> 00:07:22.875
enough events so that it continues
to push these towards the cortex.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:26.474
I don't want to get into too much
detail about how the synapses work

00:07:26.474 --> 00:07:29.984
because I don't fully understand it.
But I think what I can take away from

00:07:29.984 --> 00:07:34.740
that is that our effort in language
learning should not be focused on

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:39.690
learning rules, studying rules, the way
traditional language instruction does.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:42.810
Instead, we should be
allowing words to come in.

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:47.370
Sounds they form, connections
to meaning, to connected

00:07:47.375 --> 00:07:49.990
meaning, to synonyms, antonyms.

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:52.350
Different contexts
where we have seen them.

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:57.540
And over time, all of this stuff develops
presumably in our cortex, and we have

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:02.190
a better and better ability to use the
language. But it's not based on rules.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:06.090
So all the time we spend studying
rules, it may be interesting. Like if

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:08.970
we've had enough—and I've said this
before—if we've had enough experience

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:13.140
with the language and then the grammar
explanation comes along to summarize

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:14.790
something we're already familiar with.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:16.580
That can be satisfying.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:19.220
I'm not sure that it moves us
along very much, but it might.

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:23.150
But conversely, if you haven't had
enough experience, enough exposure,

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:29.150
then trying to generalize from a
rule is not going to be that successful,

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:31.190
And that is how we designed LingQ.

00:08:31.195 --> 00:08:35.120
And in another video, I'm going to
talk about how LingQ was designed to

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:37.490
deal with this pattern of learning.

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:41.210
Not that I had read Manfred Spitzer's
book at the time, but I just had

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:43.039
observed in my own language learning

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:46.275
the extent to which our brains
can pick up on patterns.

00:08:46.305 --> 00:08:47.535
Examples? Yes!

00:08:47.564 --> 00:08:48.225
Grammar rules?

00:08:48.225 --> 00:08:51.344
No! And that had always been my
experience in learning languages.

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:53.355
Given enough patterns and examples,

00:08:53.415 --> 00:08:55.425
gradually my brain got
used to the language.

00:08:55.515 --> 00:08:56.775
It turns out that that's right.

